module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.emitted_int_arithmetic_wraps_where_the_interpreter_refuses

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data emitted_int_arithmetic_wraps_where_the_interpreter_refuses: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "emitted_int_arithmetic_wraps_where_the_interpreter_refuses" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "emitted Int arithmetic WRAPS where the interpreter REFUSES (one accepted program, two realizations, and on every overflowing input one of them returns a fabricated value).",

    "INVALID STATE: a substrate Int operator realized on Rust as the bare i64 infix token. The interpreter evaluates + - * / % and negation with checked arithmetic and refuses with IntegerOverflow or DivisionByZero (v1.interpreter eval_int_binop, eval_unaryop); Rust's + - * and prefix - on i64 trap only under overflow-checks, which neither the workspace release profile nor any emitted Cargo.toml turns on, so in release they wrap in two's complement -- the fact extdeps.languages.rust.primitives already recorded on the i64 row as overflow: TwoComplementWrap, and nothing consulted.",

    "HARM: silent wrongness on the source-to-Rust path, outside the DESIGN section 4b ladder. It covered every emitted crate AND the seed's own generated arithmetic: after the repair 556 operator sites across 46 stage0 mirror files route through the refusing helpers (357 add, 115 sub, 43 mul, 24 div, 10 rem, 7 neg).",

    "THE INTERPRETER'S REFUSAL IS TERMINAL, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES A PANIC THE FAITHFUL REALIZATION: an operator IntegerOverflow is an Err(InterpError) propagated by ? to a failed run; no interpreter arm converts it back into a Value a program can match. The matchable form is a different API, std.checked_arithmetic CheckedInt, whose bodies apply the operators only to operands proven in range, so it is unaffected.",

    "DISCRIMINATING RED, executed 2026-09-24 on a BuildBuddy amd64 runner, emitted crate built with cargo --release: `fn bump(x: Int) -> Int { x + 1 }` at 9223372036854775807 printed -9223372036854775808 and exited 0; sub at i64::MIN, mul at 2^62*2 and negation of i64::MIN likewise wrapped. / and % by zero and i64::MIN / -1 already panicked in release, but with rustc's text rather than the interpreter's. The interpreter refused all seven with IntegerOverflow or DivisionByZero.",

    "REPAIR: one realization authority. extdeps.languages.rust.emit rust_refusing_int_operator_helper and rust_refusing_int_negation_helper name the helpers; v1.compiler.emit_rust emit_rust_host_bin_op and emit_typed_expr route every operand whose realized checkpoint is rust_refusing_int_target_type through them; v1.compiler.runtime_rust rt_checked_int_ops realizes them with checked_* and panics with the interpreter's own Display text. GREEN, same runner and harness: all seven overflowing cases refuse with the interpreter's message, and the in-range control returns 55 on both arms.",

    "COST: the regen round (claim_executor --required-regen, whole population, one runner) measured 384819 ms on the pre-repair seed and 354101 ms on the fixed-point repaired seed; the difference is inside runner noise and is not claimed as a speedup, only as no measurable regression.",

    "RESIDUE, named so the row is not read as retired: an operand whose type realizes as some integer primitive OTHER than i64 still splices the wrapping token; the Go emitter's Int operators are unexamined against the same red; and hand-written seed Rust still wraps in release because overflow-checks is off in [profile.release] -- recommended as defense-in-depth and deliberately not flipped in this change.",

    "RUNG: mechanically preventable on the source-to-Rust path by the realization rows, NOT structural: an emitter arm that splices an Int operator outside emit_rust_host_bin_op would reintroduce the class. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, as a capability: the Int operator's overflow semantics declared once in the substrate and consulted by the interpreter and by EVERY emission target's operator realization, so a target whose primitive's overflow disagrees refuses emission rather than splicing its token."
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
